WEBVTT

00:00.090 --> 00:01.950
Instructor: So, as I've said a couple times,

00:01.950 --> 00:04.230
pulls can result in conflicts.

00:04.230 --> 00:05.400
It's not a horrible thing.

00:05.400 --> 00:08.430
Just like any merge conflicts, we can resolve them.

00:08.430 --> 00:11.310
But sometimes you might have some work locally

00:11.310 --> 00:14.220
that is not on GitHub, and GitHub might have some commits

00:14.220 --> 00:15.540
that you don't have locally.

00:15.540 --> 00:18.510
So when you pull down, there may be conflicts.

00:18.510 --> 00:21.660
For example, if we're working on the master branch

00:21.660 --> 00:25.080
I've made this new change, a new commit in yellow

00:25.080 --> 00:29.400
and at the same time my collaborator Bob Head

00:29.400 --> 00:32.940
made three green commits and push them up to GitHub.

00:32.940 --> 00:35.130
When I try and pull down, right,

00:35.130 --> 00:38.400
I pull those new commits down, git pull origin master.

00:38.400 --> 00:40.320
Just like when we're merging

00:40.320 --> 00:41.910
sometimes we get a fast forward merge

00:41.910 --> 00:43.650
and things go pretty smoothly.

00:43.650 --> 00:45.120
Sometimes Git can figure out

00:45.120 --> 00:46.800
how to merge things automatically

00:46.800 --> 00:48.840
but sometimes there might be conflicts

00:48.840 --> 00:51.000
if we're changing the same files

00:51.000 --> 00:53.130
if there's, you know, conflicting changes.

00:53.130 --> 00:55.620
So when that happens, if there are conflicts

00:55.620 --> 00:58.053
then Git is going to ask us to resolve them

00:58.053 --> 01:00.840
and figure it out and commit the changes.

01:00.840 --> 01:02.550
So I'll demonstrate that,

01:02.550 --> 01:06.810
I've set up well I have the food branch,

01:06.810 --> 01:08.160
right on this food branch

01:08.160 --> 01:10.380
I have this coffee file that I added.

01:10.380 --> 01:12.540
We'll say that my friend added it

01:12.540 --> 01:14.160
and pushed it up to GitHub.

01:14.160 --> 01:15.570
I don't have it locally.

01:15.570 --> 01:16.690
If I switch to food

01:21.360 --> 01:22.920
I don't have coffee, right?

01:22.920 --> 01:26.040
I have apple, banana, and popsicle.

01:26.040 --> 01:28.500
And let's say that I decided, you know

01:28.500 --> 01:30.780
I really wanna make a coffee file in here.

01:30.780 --> 01:35.370
So I'm gonna make my own coffee, touch coffee.txt

01:35.370 --> 01:37.620
and then I'm gonna open it up

01:37.620 --> 01:39.480
and put some coffee askie art,

01:39.480 --> 01:42.000
something different than what I have on GitHub.

01:42.000 --> 01:45.574
Alright, so I paste this in just another Askie art coffee

01:45.574 --> 01:50.067
and I'm gonna add and commit that, git add coffee

01:50.067 --> 01:55.067
git commit-m, add my coffee art.

01:58.200 --> 02:00.960
So now what I wanna do is share that change

02:00.960 --> 02:03.840
that I've made and get it up on GitHub.

02:03.840 --> 02:05.370
But as a good practice

02:05.370 --> 02:08.250
before you ever push something up to GitHub

02:08.250 --> 02:11.790
you wanna pull down and see if there's changes, right?

02:11.790 --> 02:14.040
If there's anything that is gonna be problematic,

02:14.040 --> 02:15.150
any conflicts.

02:15.150 --> 02:16.620
So that's what I'm gonna do.

02:16.620 --> 02:18.900
Git Pull Origin

02:18.900 --> 02:22.080
and I want the latest changes from the food branch.

02:22.080 --> 02:24.990
But remember I have a commit on here

02:24.990 --> 02:26.640
that does not exist on GitHub.

02:26.640 --> 02:28.980
And GitHub has a commit on the food branch

02:28.980 --> 02:31.530
that does not exist on my machine.

02:31.530 --> 02:33.690
So when I do that, oh no.

02:33.690 --> 02:36.000
Who could have ever predicted this conflict

02:36.000 --> 02:39.933
in coffee txt, automatic merge failed.

02:39.933 --> 02:40.766
It doesn't know what to keep

02:40.766 --> 02:43.380
fix the conflicts and then commit the results.

02:43.380 --> 02:46.830
So this is what came from GitHub that our collaborator

02:46.830 --> 02:49.410
whoever that was, Bob Head, added.

02:49.410 --> 02:51.600
And then this is what I had locally.

02:51.600 --> 02:52.740
So do I want one?

02:52.740 --> 02:54.270
Do I want both?

02:54.270 --> 02:55.440
Let's do both.

02:55.440 --> 02:56.310
Why not?

02:56.310 --> 02:58.380
So I'll keep that.

02:58.380 --> 03:00.480
Remember, I can also use the VS code.

03:00.480 --> 03:03.180
Just accept both changes button.

03:03.180 --> 03:04.260
That makes it easy.

03:04.260 --> 03:05.400
I'll save.

03:05.400 --> 03:06.630
So I've got that.

03:06.630 --> 03:08.610
Now go back here.

03:08.610 --> 03:10.320
I need to make sure that I add the changes.

03:10.320 --> 03:13.590
So Git add coffee, Git commit

03:13.590 --> 03:16.743
and we'll just do something boring like fix merge conflicts.

03:19.440 --> 03:22.410
And now we've merged together everything

03:22.410 --> 03:24.840
but we see something that we didn't see before.

03:24.840 --> 03:27.600
Your branch is now ahead of Origin/Food.

03:27.600 --> 03:28.950
How is it ahead?

03:28.950 --> 03:31.020
Well, the reason it's ahead is

03:31.020 --> 03:34.680
that we have this commit and the merge commit

03:34.680 --> 03:36.780
and those don't exist on GitHub, right?

03:36.780 --> 03:39.090
This is why we wanna pull down first,

03:39.090 --> 03:41.160
merge anything if there are problems,

03:41.160 --> 03:44.670
and now I can push up to GitHub and there won't be conflicts

03:44.670 --> 03:47.848
unless somebody happened to change GitHub, you know

03:47.848 --> 03:50.100
add new commits or something in the meantime

03:50.100 --> 03:51.780
the last 30 seconds or so.

03:51.780 --> 03:54.120
Otherwise we'll be good to go.

03:54.120 --> 03:56.430
So origin/food is still right here.

03:56.430 --> 03:59.220
This is what GitHub looks like at this point in time.

03:59.220 --> 04:04.020
It has the original coffee.txt file on this food branch

04:04.020 --> 04:06.270
but it doesn't have my coffee commit

04:06.270 --> 04:08.580
and it doesn't have the merge commit.

04:08.580 --> 04:12.190
So all I need to do now is Git push

04:13.110 --> 04:15.693
and then I can specify origin food.

04:19.290 --> 04:21.930
And now if I head over here and refresh,

04:21.930 --> 04:24.510
we see that we have a new commit showing up,

04:24.510 --> 04:25.557
fixed merge conflicts.

04:25.557 --> 04:30.000
And if I go into coffee.txt, both of them are here.

04:30.000 --> 04:32.298
If we take a look at all the commits on this branch

04:32.298 --> 04:36.030
you can see we've got Create the Apple, okay?

04:36.030 --> 04:37.680
Create coffee.txt.

04:37.680 --> 04:39.420
That was the one we did on GitHub.

04:39.420 --> 04:41.400
Then add my coffee art,

04:41.400 --> 04:44.121
that's the one that I just did locally.

04:44.121 --> 04:45.570
And then fix merge conflicts.

04:45.570 --> 04:49.110
So that's a simple example of generating a merge conflict

04:49.110 --> 04:50.160
when you pull,

04:50.160 --> 04:52.590
some work happened that we didn't have.

04:52.590 --> 04:56.250
And at the same time we did local work that conflicted

04:56.250 --> 04:59.130
with the work that was done and pushed up to GitHub.

04:59.130 --> 05:01.530
So when I pull down there's a merge

05:01.530 --> 05:04.230
and sometimes merges result in conflicts.

05:04.230 --> 05:07.770
Alright? So that is the basics of using Git Pull.

05:07.770 --> 05:11.370
The last thing I'll show you with Pull is a shorter syntax.

05:11.370 --> 05:12.470
That's the next video.
